# MECHANISM PIN — a control that verifies loadability without polluting the KB.
#
# Fixture INLINE (mechanism pin): the subject is the ENGINE's pin runner, not any KB.
#
# THE BUG THIS CLOSES. A content pin file uses `:accept` as a CONTROL — "this rule must
# still load" — and never wants the rule afterwards. But `:accept` left it in the store,
# so every query below ran against a WIDENED base. In a real constitution suite four
# complement controls each concluded `prisoner`, and a query below them passed even
# though the rule that should derive it could have been deleted outright; a `~false`
# control made a person with no conviction at all answer TRUE for `prisoner`.
#
# `:refuse` never had this problem — a refused statement never enters the store — and
# that asymmetry between the two directives WAS the bug. The only workaround was
# ordering the file so controls came last, which is a constraint the file has to
# document and a reader has to keep honouring.
#
# `:accept-scoped` compiles and stratifies the statement, reports loadability as the
# pin verdict, then DISCARDS it. Retraction is the primitive rather than a bespoke
# undo, so this inherits `retract_diff.rs`'s pinned "retract == never-asserted".

person(Adam).

# ── THE CONTROL LOADS, AND IS THEN GONE ──────────────────────────────────────
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) -> prisoner($x).

# If the scope works nothing derives `prisoner` any more. This is the whole pin: under
# plain `:accept` the same query answers TRUE.
? prisoner(Adam).
# => FALSE

# ── AND IT TAKES NOTHING ELSE WITH IT ────────────────────────────────────────
# Discarding the control must restore the KB, not empty it.
? person(Adam).
# => TRUE

# ── PLAIN `:accept` STILL RETAINS, WHICH EXISTING FILES DEPEND ON ────────────
:accept
all $x: person($x) -> prisoner($x).

? prisoner(Adam).
# => TRUE

# ── LOADABILITY IS THE PIN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A control that stops COMPILING is a finding, not a silent pass — that is the whole
# reason a control exists. (Unstratifiable: `prisoner` reads its own negation.)
:refuse reasoning /Unstratifiable negation/
all $x: person($x) & ~prisoner($x) -> prisoner($x).

:expect-pins 6
